Let's use the angle bisector theorem. We have \( \overline{CD} \) as the angle bisector, dividing \( \overline{AB} \) into segments \( \overline{AD} \) and \( \overline{DB} \) such that \( \frac{AD}{DB} = \frac{AC}{BC} \). Since \( \cos \frac{C}{2} = \frac{1}{3} \), we can use the identity \( \cos^2 \frac{C}{2} = \frac{1+\cos C}{2} \) to find \( \cos C \):
\begin{align*}
\frac{1}{9} &= \frac{1+\cos C}{2} \\
\frac{2}{9} &= 1 + \cos C \\
\cos C &= -\frac{7}{9}.
\end{align*}
Thus, \( \sin C \) can be found using the Pythagorean identity \( \sin^2 C + \cos^2 C = 1 \):
\begin{align*}
\sin^2 C &= 1 - \left(-\frac{7}{9}\right)^2 \\
\sin^2 C &= 1 - \frac{49}{81} \\
\sin^2 C &= \frac{32}{81} \\
\sin C &= \frac{4\sqrt{2}}{9}.
\end{align*}

Applying the length of the angle bisector formula, which is \( CD = \frac{2 \cdot AC \cdot BC \cdot \cos \frac{C}{2}}{AC + BC} \), we substitute \( \cos \frac{C}{2} = \frac{1}{3} \) and \( CD = 6 \):
\begin{align*}
6 &= \frac{2 \cdot AC \cdot BC \cdot \frac{1}{3}}{AC + BC} \\
18(AC + BC) &= 2 \cdot AC \cdot BC \\
9(AC + BC) &= AC \cdot BC \\
\end{align*}
Now, we have \( 9(AC + BC) = AC \cdot BC \). We want to find \( \frac{1}{BC} + \frac{1}{AC} \):
\begin{align*}
\frac{1}{BC} + \frac{1}{AC} &= \frac{AC + BC}{AC \cdot BC} \\
\end{align*}
Since \( 9(AC + BC) = AC \cdot BC \), we can substitute \( AC \cdot BC \) with \( 9(AC + BC) \):
\begin{align*}
\frac{1}{BC} + \frac{1}{AC} &= \frac{AC + BC}{9(AC + BC)} \\
&= \frac{1}{9}.
